For comparisons with baseline models on downstream tasks, we set $m_{\text{r}}=0.60$, $L_{\text{enc}}=12$, $L_{\text{dec}}=4$, $d_{\text{e}}=64$, and $(N_{\text{p,s}}, N_{\text{p,f}}) = (1, 16)$. These choices result in 31\% fewer encoder parameters and 7\% fewer total parameters compared with LWM (see Table~\ref{tab:params}). For LWM, we use publicly available pretrained weights and the code to generate its embeddings \cite{lwm} without retraining or any modifications.


\subsection{Pretraining of ContraWiMAE}

We warm-start the encoder and decoder components of ContraWiMAE by copying the corresponding weights from WiMAE to preserve its reconstruction capability. We use the same learning rate schedule, optimizer, and batch size for continual pretraining of ContraWiMAE on the same pretraining dataset. To maximize the transferability of the reconstruction capability, we use $m_{\text{r}}=0.60$, $L_{\text{enc}}=12$, $L_{\text{dec}}=4$, $d_{\text{e}}=64$, and $(N_{\text{p,s}}, N_{\text{p,f}}) = (1, 16)$, matching the parameters for WiMAE used in downstream task evaluations. We set the reconstructive and contrastive loss weights to $\alpha=0.9$ and $1-\alpha=0.1$, respectively, using a temperature parameter $\tau=0.1$. We also set the contrastive space dimension to $d_{\text{c}}=64$ and uniformly sample the noise variance for positive pairs to achieve channel SNR values between 20\,dB and 30\,dB.


