All the experiments in this work were conducted with the help of AutoDRIVE Ecosystem\footnote{\textbf{Website:} \url{https://autodrive-ecosystem.github.io}} \cite{AutoDRIVE2023, AutoDRIVEReport2022}. Particularly, AutoDRIVE Simulator \cite{AutoDRIVESimulator2021, AutoDRIVESimulatorReport2020} was employed to simulate the autonomy-oriented digital twins \cite{DT-Across-Scales-2024} of Nigel (small-scale, on-road), RoboRacer (small-scale, racing), and RZR (large-scale, off-road) to collect training and testing data for the various ablation studies. Additionally, AutoDRIVE Testbed (with Nigel \cite{Nigel-4WD4WS-2024}) was used for the end-to-end autonomous driving case study (refer Fig. \ref{fig4}).

\subsubsection{Ablation Studies}

We performed ablation studies in 4 stages. The first set of experiments was meant to qualitatively assess the effect of (i) domain adaptation direction \{sim2real, real2sim\}, (ii) foundation model \{SDXL, SDXL-Turbo\}, (iii) denoising steps \{1, 5, 30\}, and (iv) input resolution \{640$\times$274, 1280$\times$548, 2560$\times$1096\}. The second, third, and fourth sets of experiments were meant to assess the effect of (i) fine-tuning and (ii) image prompting, qualitatively and quantitatively. To this end, we fine-tuned the SD1.5 foundation model on 3 custom concepts (refer Fig. \ref{fig3}) using the DreamBooth approach \cite{DreamBooth2023}. The fine-tuning comprised a \textit{``trigger word''} of the form \texttt{<sim\_scale\_odd>} to identify/associate a particular concept with a particular simulator, scale, and operational design domain (ODD).

\subsubsection{Performance Study}

We analyzed the performance of the fine-tuned latent diffusion model conditioned via text as well as image prompts. This experiment involved training and inference of the sim2real diffusion model on (i) Palmetto Cluster (8 CPU cores, 64 GB RAM) with 4 different GPU models \{V100, A100, H100, L40S\}, (ii) Google Colab (2 CPU cores, 12 GB RAM) with T4 GPU, and (iii) a laptop PC (20 CPU cores, 32 GB RAM) with 3080 Ti GPU.

\subsubsection{Case Study}

The chosen case study builds on \cite{RBC2021}, aiming to clone the end-to-end driving behavior of a human driver using a six-layer convolutional neural network (CNN). AutoDRIVE Simulator was employed for collecting 5 laps worth of manual driving data ($\sim$1700 samples), which was balanced, normalized, and resized without any augmentation. The CNN model was trained to predict the actuator commands corresponding to the given camera frame for 4 epochs at a learning rate of 1e-3, which resulted in stable convergence. The simulation environment consisted of a figure-8 track with a black road on green vinyl mat surrounded by white boxes. The road had white lane markings, and the intersection had marked crosswalks. Contrarily, the real-world environment consisted of a rather irregular track created using black tissues (textures, flat edges, folds) laid out on a green table cloth (folds, creases, reflections) surrounded by brown cardboard boxes (damage, imprints, tapes), which exaggerated the sim2real gap. Additionally, the camera model in simulation (Raspberry Pi Camera V2) and reality (Intel RealSense D435i) were different, resulting in a perception gap due to film grain, exposure, resolution, and field of view (FOV) of the perceived image.

\subsubsection{Evaluation Metrics}

The following metrics were used to assess the quality and performance of the proposed sim2real diffusion framework:

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Feature Similarity:} Feature similarity is evaluated by encoding the input and output images using the CLIP-ViT-L/14 encoder and computing the cosine similarity (CS) of the resulting embedding vectors.
    \begin{multline}
    \label{Cosine-Similarity}
    \mathrm{CS}(A, B) = \cos(\theta) = \frac{\mathbf{A} \cdot \mathbf{B}}{ \left\| \mathbf{A} \right\| \left\| \mathbf{B} \right\|} \\ = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} A_i B_i}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} A_i^2} \cdot \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} B_i^2}}
    \end{multline}
    Cosine similarity ranges from -1 (perfect dissimilarity) to 1 (perfect similarity), with 0 indicating no similarity. It focuses on the relative pattern of features (e.g., shapes, textures, or objects) and ignores anything that only affects the magnitude of the vectors (e.g., lighting or contrast).

    \item \textbf{Context Similarity:} CLIP directional similarity (CLIP-DS) evaluates the contextual alignment between the \{input image, original caption\} and \{output image, modified caption\}. It maps both images and captions into a common high-dimensional space, where similar concepts are close together, and computes the cosine similarity of the resulting embedding vectors. This metric ranges from -1 (perfect dissimilarity) to 1 (perfect similarity) and focuses on the relative pattern of contextual features (e.g., shapes or textures in images, and words or phrases in text).

    \item \textbf{Content Difference:} Content difference is evaluated using learned perceptual image patch similarity (LPIPS) \cite{LPIPS2018}, which is a deep-learning-based perceptual metric that measures how perceptually similar two images are by computing the $L_2$ distance between their feature activations at multiple layers of AlexNet \cite{AlexNet2012}.
    \begin{multline}
    \label{LPIPS}
    \mathrm{LPIPS}(x,y) = \sum_{l} \frac{1}{H_l \cdot W_l} \sum_{h,w} \\ \left\| w_l \odot \left( \phi^l(x)_{h,w} - \phi^l(y)_{h,w} \right) \right\|_2^2
    \end{multline}
    LPIPS score ranges from 0 (perfect similarity) to 1 (completely different). It evaluates perceptual quality at a high level (texture, details, global image structure).

    \item \textbf{Style Difference:} Neural style loss is evaluated using Gram matrix difference, which measures the difference in texture and style between two images. It is computed as the mean squared error between Gram matrices of feature maps (typically from VGG \cite{VGG2015} layers).
    \begin{multline}
    \label{SD}
    \mathrm{SD}(x, s) = \sum_{l \in L} w_l \left\| \frac{F^l_x (F^l_x)^\top}{C_l H_l W_l} - \frac{F^l_s (F^l_s)^\top}{C_l H_l W_l} \right\|_F^2
    \end{multline}
    We compute the style difference between (i) input-style pair (SD-IS), and (ii) output-style pair (SD-OS), in order to establish the relative reduction in style difference.

    \item \textbf{Performance:} Iterations per second (IPS) is a performance measure to assess the denoising rate. It measures the number of denoising steps $\mathrm{n_\textrm{iter}}$ completed within a predefined time interval $\Delta T$.
    \begin{align}
    \label{IPS}
    \mathrm{IPS} = \frac{\mathrm{n_{iter}}}{\Delta T}
    \end{align}
    IPS values can range from 0 (no update) to upwards of 6 (sufficient for low-speed applications), with $>$30 IPS being excellent for general-purpose autonomy.
\end{itemize}

