The second ablation study (refer Fig. \ref{fig6} and Table \ref{tab1}) was performed to assess the effects of fine-tuning and image-prompting on latent diffusion models. Similar to the earlier study, a common set of input (real) and prompt (sim) frames were provided to the diffusion model(s) to assess their domain adaptation capabilities across a range of experiments:

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Image-Prompting:} Image prompt (IP) provides additional conditioning, which significantly aids in domain adaptation (compare Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(c)} with Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(d)}, or Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(e)} with Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(f)}). It was observed that, as opposed to the vanilla models, those conditioned via an image prompt performed better across all the quantitative metrics (compare Table \ref{tab1} rows 1 and 2, or 3 and 4), albeit adding a small processing overhead.

    \item \textbf{Fine-Tuning:} Fine-tuning teaches the foundation model a custom concept, which serves a dual purpose. Firstly, it improves the qualitative (compare Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(c)} with Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(e)}, or Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(d)} with Fig. \hyperref[fig6]{\ref*{fig6}(f)}) and quantitative (compare Table \ref{tab1} rows 1 and 3, or 2 and 4) performance of domain adaptation. Secondly, fine-tuning using a \textit{``trigger word''} for each concept makes this approach highly modular and scalable across novel domain gaps. Finally, combining fine-tuning with image-prompting usually results in the most effective domain adaptation, as marked by the highest (44.41\%) reduction in style difference.

\end{itemize}

