\section{Discrete Differential Forms and the Exterior Derivative}\label{sec:DiscreteForms}
Having learned about chains and the boundary homomorphism we can now define cochains and the coboundary operator on them, which correspond to our \textit{discrete differential forms} and the \textit{discrete exterior derivative}.

\begin{definition}\label{def:cochain}
 Let $K$ be a pseudomanifold and $C_n(K)$ the group of oriented $n$-chains of $K$. Let $G$ be an abelian group. The group of \textbf{\textit{n}-dimensional cochains} of $K$, with coefficients in $G$, is the group
 \[C^n(K) =\text{ Hom }(C_n(K),G). \]
 The \textbf{coboundary operator} $d$ is defined to be the dual of the boundary operator $\partial_n: C_{n+1}(K) \rightarrow C_{n}(K)$, i.e., it is the homomorphism
 \[ d: C^n(K) \rightarrow C^{n+1}(K),\]
 such that $dd = 0.$
\end{definition}
We can think of a cochain group $C^n(K)$ as dual of a chain group $C_n(K)$. We now give the formal definition of discrete differential forms and the discrete exterior derivative:

\begin{definition}\label{def:DForm}
 A \textbf{discrete \textit{q}--form} $\alpha^q$ on a pseudomanifold $K$ is an element of $C^q(K)$, the group of \textit{q}--dimensional cochains of $K$, that is
 \[ \alpha^q \in C^q(K) =\text{ Hom }(C_q(K),G). \]
 The \textbf{discrete exterior derivative} $d_q: C^q(K) \rightarrow C^{q+1}(K)$ is the coboundary operator and it holds:
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:derivative}
  (d_q\alpha^q)(c_{q+1}) = \alpha(\partial_{q+1} c_{q+1}) = \sum_{c\in C_q(K)} [c_{q+1}:c]\alpha(c).
 \end{equation}
\end{definition}

The discrete exterior derivative is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig1}.
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{fig1.png}
\caption{
Boundary homomorphism and exterior derivative on polygonal complex. In this figure, our pseudomanifold $K$ has 2--chain
$C_2 = (f_0, f_1)$, 1--chain $C_1 = (e_0,\dots,e_5$), and 0--chain $C_0 = (v_0,\dots,v_4)$. If 2--form $\gamma^2$ was given as the exterior derivative of 1--form $\beta^1$, then $\gamma = d\beta$ would be defined per faces $f_0, f_1$ by equation (\ref{eq:derivative}). Concretely $\gamma(f_1) = d\beta(f_1)$ is:\\
$
(d\beta)(f_1) = \beta(\partial f_1)
= \sum_{e_i \in C_1} [f_1:e_i]\beta(e_i)
= 0\cdot \beta(e_0) - 1\cdot \beta(e_1) + 0\cdot \beta(e_2)
+ 1\cdot \beta(e_3) + 1\cdot \beta(e_4) + 1\cdot \beta(e_5).
$
}\label{fig1}
\end{figure}

Since now on, we work with coefficient group $G = (\mathbb{R},+)$, which corresponds to working with \textbf{real--valued discrete differential forms}.

Similarly to the definition of chain complexes (Definition \ref{def:ChainComplex}), we have the following:
\begin{definition}\label{def:CochainComplex}
A \textbf{cochain complex} $(C^\ast(K,\Re),d)$ is a sequence of abelian groups of $n$-dimensional cochains $C^n(K)$ together with the coboundary operators
$d_n:C^n(K) \rightarrow C^{n+1}(K)$, i.e.,
\[0 \overset{d_n}{\longleftarrow} C^n(K) \overset{d_{n-1}}{\longleftarrow} \cdots \overset{d_{k}}{\longleftarrow} C^k(K) \overset{d_{k-1}}{\longleftarrow} \cdots \overset{d_0}{\longleftarrow} C^0(K) \longleftarrow 0.\]

 A $q$--form $\alpha$ is said to be a \textbf{closed form} (cocycle) if $d\alpha = 0$, and it is called an \textbf{exact form} (coboundary) if there exist a $(q-1)$--form $\beta$ such that $d\beta =\alpha$.

Note that each exact $q$-form $\alpha = d\beta$ is closed, since $d\alpha = dd\beta=0$. Two closed $q$-forms are \textbf{cohomologous} if they differ by an exact $q$--form. We denote cohomology classes by $H^\ast$:
 \[H^q(C^\ast)=\frac{\ker d_q(C^\ast)}{\im d_{q-1}(C^\ast)}.\]
\end{definition}




