% \]
% where $i, j \in [s]$. It seems plausible that $\deg(f) = (m-1)s^2$ when $s > 1$, and if not, we can consider $\Phi(f)$ for a random function $\Phi\colon B \to B$. Conversely, the usual argument extends to show that $s(f) \leq (m-1)s$. Indeed, consider a point $x$ with $f(x) = b$. If changing a coordinate increases $f$ then it must be the unique minimal coordinate in some block, and this coordinate must be increased to more than $b$. This gives at most $(m - b)s$ options. If changing a coordinate decreases $f$ then it must be the unique maximal tribe, and it must be decreased to less than $b$. This gives at most $(b - 1)s$ options, for a total of $(m - 1)s$. Hence
