\begin{abstract}

This paper examines the impact of agents' myopic optimization on the efficiency of systems comprised by many selfish agents. In contrast to standard 
congestion games where agents interact in a one-shot fashion, in our model each agent chooses an {\em infinite sequence} of actions and maximizes the total reward stream {\em discounted over time} under different ways of computing present values. 
%Actions consume resources with finite availability that get congested, and their completion time depends on the number of agents that execute different actions.
Our model assumes that actions consume common resources that get congested, and the action choice by an agent affects the completion times of actions chosen by other agents, which in turn affects the time rewards are accrued and their discounted value.
%The completion time of each action depends on \CC{{\em congestion}} the distribution of the agents over the different actions in a general way, such that only {\em Little's law} and {\em rate monotonicity} are assumed to hold.
%The present value of each reward obtained by completing an action depends on when it is accrued; rewards obtained farther in the future have lower present values
This is a {\em mean-field game}, where an agent's reward depends on the decisions of the other agents through the resulting action completion times. For this type of game we define stationary equilibria, and analyze their existence and {\em price of anarchy} (PoA). 

%In this context, we use a similar formulation as in mean field games to 
Overall, we find that the PoA depends entirely on the type of discounting rather than its specific parameters. 
For {\em exponential discounting}, myopic behaviour leads to extreme inefficiency: the PoA is $\infty$ for any value of the discount parameter. %Explicit PoA expressions are given in terms of appropriately defined indices for each action.
For {\em power law discounting}, such inefficiency is greatly reduced and the PoA is 2 whenever stationary equilibria exist. This matches the PoA when there is no discounting and players maximize long-run average rewards.  %Interestingly, this is the same PoA as in the case when agents maximize their long run average reward.
Additionally, we observe that exponential discounting may introduce unstable equilibria in learning algorithms, if action completion times are interdependent.
In contrast, under no discounting all equilibria are stable.
\end{abstract}



