\subsection{Related Work}
For continuous time and state space, mean field games were introduced by \cite{lions} and \cite{huang}, but
it is only recently that mean field games in finite state and action spaces have been studied in sufficient generality, e.g., see \cite{neumann}. %Although exponential discounting is considered, the discount factor is constant.
%For example, employs standard fixed-point arguments to demonstrate the existence of stationary equilibria in such games. 
%However, previous work \emph{has not
%addressed the effect of discounting on efficiency}. %In this work we provide explicit expressions for the PoA and investigate the effect of discounting for a specific class of mean field games where each nonatomic agent has to repeatedly choose among a common set of actions. Each action has a sojourn time to complete, which depends on the distribution of the mass of the rest of the agents over the set of actions. 
 The existing literature is primarily focused in existence of equilibria and only a few works tackle efficiency \cite{carmona_poa,tomlin,cd23}.

 The authors in \cite{cd23} established the existence, uniqueness, and PoA properties of stationary equilibria of a mean field game with queueing interactions  similar to the model in this paper. The calculation of the equilibrium is reduced to the optimal solutions of a convex program. In this paper,  the above model  is extended by considering agents that use general types of discounting and more general queueing interactions with nondecreasing time delay function. This requires the development of different proof techniques, since the methods used in the analysis in \cite{cd23} are particular to the case of average rewards and do not extend to the discounted case.
%We believe that the methods and arguments in this paper can be extended to a wider range of MFGs  as we mention in the conclusions.

The stateless setting of our model looks similar to the mean-field multiarmed bandit games \cite{Gummadi2013} and  stateless mean-field games \cite{Yardim2023}, where the rewards obtained by an agent depend on the number of agents choosing the same arm/action. In these models, the time and reward are essentially decoupled and, hence there is no temporal interaction.  In our model, the distribution of agents determines when and how often an agent receives a reward. 
%Our research places emphasis on examining the continuous temporal interaction among agents.

%Our model, and MFGs more generally, are also related to anonymous games comprised by a continuum of agents, e.g., see \cite{JOVANOVIC198877,piotr2015}. There too, efficiency is not studied.
The PoA has been extensively studied in continuous congestion games (a subclass of anonymous games). Roughgarden and Tardos  investigated the PoA of selfish routing games in their work \cite{roughgarden}. Later, Roughgarden developed the smoothness argument \cite{roughgarden2009}, which has been used to determine the PoA in splittable congestion games \cite{roughgarden2015}. However, the analysis approach in those papers is not applicable in our setup, since we consider a dynamic game where agents make decisions over time instead of a static setting. Furthermore, these results heavily rely on the assumption of polynomial cost functions, and as a result, the PoA depends on the degree of nonlinearity, i.e., the degree of the polynomial. In our approach, we analyze the degree of myopicness among agents with general utility functions. To be able to do that, we had to develop a different method to compute the PoA.

Extensive research has been conducted on the  equilibrium behavior under discounting in  repeated games \cite{dilip1988,drew1986} and dynamic games \cite{KARP2007557,neyman2003,adlakha}. The primary focus of these studies was on investigating the existence and uniqueness of equilibrium. In contrast, our work goes beyond existence, uniqueness and examines efficiency aspects.
The consideration of different types of discounting also touches on the issue of consistency of time preferences, the subject of an extensive economics literature, e.g., see \cite{peter1982} and references therein\footnote{Note that Bellman's principle of optimality may not hold in dynamic games with nonexponential (i.e., inconsistent) discounting.}. The concept of stationary equilibrium, which is often used as a solution concept for mean field games, may not be suitable in these cases. To address this issue, various methods have been developed, including sensitivity-based approaches \cite{cao} and the extended Bellman system \cite{tomas}.
Aiming for simplicity, we opted to confine ourselves to stationary equilibria, thus recognizing that in some cases these may not exist.
\end{comment}
The paper is organized as follows.
