\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:little_law}
x_i(\boldsymbol{\mu}) = 
    \frac{\mu_i}{\tau_i(\boldsymbol{\mu})},
\end{equation}
on $\mathcal{U}_m(A)$, and let $\boldsymbol{x}(\boldsymbol{\mu}) = (x_i(\boldsymbol{\mu}))_{i\in A}$. 
This condition expresses Little's law: at any time, the average mass of agents in a service facility where the action is executed, is equal to the average rate of agents entering the facility  multiplied by the average time an agent spends in the facility. 
Thus,
$x_i(\boldsymbol{\mu})$ is interpreted as the rate (in mass per unit of time) of agents executing action $i\in A$.
