\vspace{-10pt}
\section{Conclusion and discussion}\label{lab:conclusion}
We introduced TensorRL-QAS, a scalable framework for quantum architecture search that combines tensor network initialization with reinforcement learning. Leveraging a matrix product state initialization, TensorRL-QAS reduces function evaluations by $100\times$ and achieves $98\%$ faster episodes compared to previous QAS approaches~\cite{he2024training, patel2024curriculum, zhang2022differentiable, liu2018darts, lu2023qas, kundu2024enhancing}, without sacrificing accuracy. For 6- to-12-qubit chemical Hamiltonians, it consistently yields circuits with $10$--$13\times$ fewer CNOTs and depth, robust performance across random seeds, and superior efficiency and stability relative to RL and non-RL baselines. The approach generalizes to non-chemical tasks, supports CPU-only training up to 8-qubit, and maintains high accuracy under amplified noise, achieving up to $38\%$ lower energy error and $2.4\times$ smaller circuit depth than leading noisy-QAS methods. These results position TensorRL-QAS as a robust, efficient, and noise-adaptive solution for quantum circuit discovery on near-term devices. 

% We next discuss limitations and interesting directions for future work. We have considered two different models for TensorRL-QAS, the difference being that in one case, the warm-start is part of the RL-state, and hence also trainable by the agent, and in the other, it is not. It would be interesting to study other combinations where, for example, the agent sees the warm-start circuit, but it cannot modify its parameters. Additionally, in this work, we have restricted the action space to single-qubit rotations about the \texttt{RX}, \texttt{RY}, and \texttt{RZ} axes and the \texttt{CNOT} gate. As the number of qubits increases and problem complexity grows, it may become necessary to design a more expressive action space. In particular, incorporating problem-specific (e.g., Hamiltonian-inspired) operations~\cite{grimsley2019adaptive, glos2024generic, moflic2024constant} or leveraging symmetries and repeated structures (such as gadgets~\cite{kundu2024easy, olle2025scaling}) could significantly improve scalability and computational tractability beyond 12-qubit. In Appendix.~\ref{appendix:scalability_beyond_twelve} we elaborate on the limitation of the \texttt{RX}, \texttt{RY}, and \texttt{RZ}, \texttt{CNOT} action and discuss its scalability and limitations up to 20-qubit quantum problem. Regarding the structure of the warm-start circuit, in our experiments, we have always used a brickwork circuit, reflecting both the linear connectivity of most currently available QPUs and the linear structure of the MPS chain. Future works could explore the impact of using both different circuit ansatz and also other tensor network structures~\cite{bilkis2023semi, HaghshenasTN-QC-2022}.
We next outline limitations and future directions. We studied two TensorRL-QAS models: one where the warm-start is part of the RL state (and thus agent-trainable), and one where it is not. Exploring intermediate variants-e.g., where the agent observes but cannot modify the warm-start circuit- remains open. Our action space was limited to single-qubit \texttt{RX}, \texttt{RY}, \texttt{RZ} rotations and \texttt{CNOT} gates; as qubit count and problem complexity grow, richer action spaces may be required. Incorporating problem-specific (e.g., Hamiltonian-inspired) gates~\cite{grimsley2019adaptive, glos2024generic, moflic2024constant} or exploiting symmetries and repeated structures (e.g., gadgets~\cite{kundu2024easy, olle2025scaling}) could improve scalability beyond 12-qubit. Appendix~\ref{appendix:scalability_beyond_twelve} discusses the scalability limits of our current action set-up to 20-qubit. Finally, we always used a brickwork warm-start circuit, matching typical QPU connectivity and MPS structure; future work could examine alternative ansatzes and tensor network layouts~\cite{bilkis2023semi, HaghshenasTN-QC-2022}.

Ultimately, due to long queue times on real QPUs, our experiments rely on noise simulations, where we artificially amplify noise by $5$--$10\times$ to test the robustness of TensorRL-QAS. However, we have not conducted training on actual quantum hardware. Future work will focus on real-device training and on developing methods to adapt TensorRL-QAS to actual QPU noise.

