\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclude}


We address the problem of limiting global energy budgets for FL using heterogeneous Jetson edge accelerators over non-IID data. Our unique bi-level ILP formulation leverages approximate Shapley values and energy-time prediction models to efficiently solve this problem. Our \method framework achieves superior training accuracies compared to SOTA and baselines for diverse energy budgets and realistic experiment configurations. In future work, we plan to run more extensive experiments on physical edge clusters and complement the ILP-based approach with a faster probabilistic heuristic that continues to balance per-round times and global model quality. We aim to extend our approach to more heterogeneous platforms, including mobile devices and accelerators like TPUs and FPGAs. We further plan on performing in-depth real-world validation to assess the impact of network conditions, client dropouts and map energy usage to CO$_2$ emissions to better assess the environmental impact of our methodology. 


