\subsection{Kernel Factorization}

Our compression framework begins by constructing hierarchical partition trees on the rows and columns of the input matrix using Algorithm~\ref{alg:questionnaire}, based on user-specified metrics. Based on the final dual affinities \( W_{\mathcal{T}_X} \) and \( W_{\mathcal{T}_Y} \), these trees are then reordered using the data-driven space-filling curve procedure described in Algorithm~\ref{alg:affinity_ordering}, which aligns the matrix structure with the intrinsic geometry of the data.

We then apply either the butterfly algorithm or eGHWT to the reordered matrix to obtain a compressed representation.
For the butterfly algorithm, the compression is performed via ID maintaining a precision of given \( \varepsilon \). 
For eGHWT, compression is achieved by retaining the largest transform coefficients such that the total approximation error remains below a user-defined threshold \( \varepsilon \). In this work, we measure the approximation error using the Frobenius norm \( \|\cdot\|_F \), as detailed in the following procedure.
The complete compression process is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:compression}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Geometry-Adaptive Kernel Factorization}
\label{alg:compression}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Input 
Discrete matrix of kernel \( \mathcal{K} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_Y \times N_X} \); error parameter \( \varepsilon \in (0,1) \); initial affinity \( W_X \); compression option: \texttt{"butterfly"} or \texttt{"eGHWT"}

\State
Use Algorithm~\ref{alg:questionnaire} with initial affinity $W_X$ to iteratively build hierarchical partition trees \( \mathcal{T}_X \) and \( \mathcal{T}_Y \) and the corresponding dual affinities \( W_{\mathcal{T}_X} \) and \( W_{\mathcal{T}_Y} \).

\State  
Apply Algorithm~\ref{alg:affinity_ordering} to determine the leaf orderings of \( \mathcal{T}_X \) and \( \mathcal{T}_Y \) using the computed affinities.

\State  
Reorder \( \mathcal{K} \) according to the new tree-based orderings.

\If {compression option == \texttt{"butterfly"}}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Apply the butterfly factorization to the reordered matrix using interpolative decomposition (ID) with precision \( \varepsilon \).
        \item Output: Final-layer skeleton matrix \( B^{(L)} \) and interpolation matrices \( \{P^{(\ell)}\} \).
    \end{itemize}
\ElsIf {compression option == \texttt{"eGHWT"}}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Apply the extended Generalized Haar-Walsh Transform (eGHWT) to the reordered matrix.
        \item Select best tiling \( \mathcal{B}^* \) and corresponding best basis \( \{\omega_{(q,p)}\}_{(q,p) \in \mathcal{B}^*} \), with coefficients:
        \begin{equation}
            c_{(q,p)} := \langle \mathcal{K}, \omega_{(q,p)} \rangle.
        \end{equation}
        \item Sort \( |c_{(q,p)}| \) in descending order as \( \{c_{(q_i,p_i)}\}_{i=1}^{N_Y N_X} \) with corresponding \( \{\omega_{(q_i,p_i)}\} \). Retain the top \( n \) terms such that:
        \begin{equation}
            \frac{\sqrt{\sum_{i=n+1}^{N_Y N_X} c_{(q_i,p_i)}^2}}{\norm{\mathcal{K}}_F} \leq \varepsilon.
        \end{equation}
        \item Output: Compressed coefficients \( \{c_{(q_i,p_i)}\}_{i=1}^n \) and basis functions \( \{\omega_{(q_i,p_i)}\}_{i=1}^n \).
    \end{itemize}
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\subsection{Fast Kernel Application}\label{section_kerenl_app}

We assume throughout this discussion that \( N_X, N_Y = O(N) \). In Algorithm~\ref{alg:compression}, if the compression option \texttt{"butterfly"} is selected, the fast kernel application is simply 
\begin{equation}\label{eq_BF_apply}
  \widehat{\mathcal K}f(y_i) = \sum_{j_1} B^{(L)}_{i, j_{L-1}}
\sum_{j_2} P^{(L-1)}_{j_{L-1}, j_{L-2}}
\cdots
\sum_{j} P^{(0)}_{j_{0}, j} f(x_{j}) .
\end{equation}
Provided that low-rank structure is successfully captured at each hierarchical level, the computational complexity of this procedure is \( O(N \log N) \).

If the compression option \texttt{"eGHWT"} is selected, the kernel matrix \( \mathcal{K}(x, y) \) is approximated using a best-basis representation
\[
w_{(q_i, p_i)}(x, y) = w_{q_i}(x) w_{p_i}(y),
\]
indexed by \( (q_i, p_i) \in \mathcal{B}^* \), along with the corresponding GHWT coefficients \( c_{(q_i, p_i)} \) for \( i = 1, \ldots, N_Y N_X \). To reduce storage and computational cost, only the top \( n \) coefficients are retained, which sufficiently satisfy a prescribed error \( \varepsilon \).
Given an input vector \( f \in \mathbb{R}^{N_X} \), interpreted as a function on the discrete domain \( [0, N_X) \), we compute its GHWT expansion coefficients:
\[
c_p^f := \langle f, w_p \rangle,
\]
which can be obtained in \( O(N_X \log N_X) \) time. The matrix-vector multiplication is then performed in the transform domain by aggregating over the retained basis pairs:
\[
\widehat{c}_{q_i} := \sum_{\substack{p_i \text{ such that } \\ (q_i, p_i) \in \mathcal{B}^*}} c_{(q_i, p_i)} \cdot c_{p_i}^f, \quad i = 1, \ldots, n,
\]
followed by reconstruction of the output vector via the inverse GHWT:
\begin{equation}\label{eq_GHWT_apply}
\widehat{\mathcal{K}}f(y) := \sum_{\substack{p_i \text{ such that } \\ (q_i, p_i) \in \mathcal{B}^*}} \widehat{c}_{q_i} \cdot w_{q_i}(y).
\end{equation}
When \( n \) is of \(O(N \log N) \) or smaller, the total complexity of this procedure is \( O(N \log N) \), offering a significant computational advantage over the \( O(N^2) \) cost of direct matrix-vector multiplication.

\section{Evaluation}
We evaluate the performance of our proposed compression framework on synthetic kernel matrices. Specifically, we assess memory usage, approximation accuracy, and computational efficiency on randomly sampled domains and geometry-organized matrices.  All entries of the matrices are stored in \texttt{Float64} format, corresponding to the standard 64-bit IEEE 754 double-precision floating-point representation. 
The butterfly and eGHWT factorizations are tested under fixed precision, and results are reported as a function of matrix size.

In each of the following tables, the column labeled \( N \) indicates the number of columns in the matrix to which our proposed algorithm is applied. The number of rows is specified separately in each numerical example. The columns labeled “Matrix Memory” report the memory usage of the original, unfactorized kernel matrix, measured in megabytes. The columns labeled “Compression” report the memory usage after applying the factorization, also in megabytes, under various matrix organizations as described in the corresponding table.
The columns labeled “Direct Evaluation” list the time, in seconds, required to perform a standard dense matrix–vector multiplication. The columns labeled “Fast Evaluation” report the time required to apply the factorized kernel using the fast algorithms described in Section~\ref{section_kerenl_app}.
The “Relative \( \ell_2 \) Error” column reports the accuracy of the kernel application. Given a test function \( f \in \mathbb{R}^N \) and the approximate image \( \widehat{\mathcal{K}}f \), obtained using either the butterfly method~\eqref{eq_bf_apply} or the eGHWT method~\eqref{eq_GHWT_apply}, the error is defined as $ \frac{\norm{\mathcal{K}f - \widehat{\mathcal{K}}f}_{\ell_2}}{\norm{\mathcal{K}f}_{\ell_2}}$.
This metric is computed using test functions drawn uniformly at random from the interval \( [0, 1] \), and the results are averaged over 100 trials.

\section{Numerical Examples}

\subsection{Eigenfunctions on $\mathbb{S}^1$}\label{sec_sine}
The functions $\sin(2\pi kx)$ and $\cos(2\pi kx)$ form the classical Fourier basis and arise as eigenfunctions of the Laplacian on the one-dimensional torus $\mathbb{S}^1$.
In this work, we use sine functions as a representative example; similar results hold for cosine functions as well. Let \( k = 1, \dots, N \). To avoid aliasing, we sample \( 2N \) points \( x_1, \dots, x_{2N} \in [0,1] \). We define the discretized kernel \( \mathcal{K} \) with entries
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{K}_{ki} = \mathcal{K}(k, x_i) = \sin(2\pi k x_i),
\end{equation}
where each column corresponds to a sampled eigenfunction.
As shown in \cite{coifman2011harmonic}, when the sample points \( x_i \) are equally spaced and the affinity between rows or columns is defined using the correlation-based tree affinity in \eqref{eq:corr}, the iterative coupled geometry algorithm can recover the latent structure of these eigenfunctions, revealing their natural ordering.
In our setting, we consider the case in which the sample points \( x_i \) are drawn non-equispaced. 

\textit{Geometry Learning.} Denote the domains of space and frequency as $X = \{x_i\}_{i=1}^{2N}$ and $K = \{k\}_{k=1}^N$.
We apply Algorithm~\ref{alg:questionnaire} to the permuted domains to recover the coupled geometry of its rows and columns. The initial affinity matrix \( W_K \) is computed using cosine similarity, and the multiscale tree $\mathcal{T}_X$ and  $\mathcal{T}_K$
tree-based affinities \( W_{\mathcal{T}_X}^{\mathrm{corr}} \) and \( W_{\mathcal{T}_K}^{\mathrm{corr}} \) are used to guide the construction of the hierarchical partitions. 
Figure~\ref{fig:sin_aff} compares the initial cosine similarity matrix $W_K$ with the learned affinity matrix \( W_{\mathcal{T}_K}^{\mathrm{corr}} \). The initial cosine similarity yields a nearly diagonal matrix, capturing only trivial self-similarities and failing to reveal meaningful relationships between different frequencies. In contrast, the tree-based affinity captures correlations across scales and uncovers the latent structure among the frequency components. 
Algorithm~\ref{alg:affinity_ordering} is applied to both \( W_{\mathcal{T}_X}^{\mathrm{corr}} \) and \( W_{\mathcal{T}_K}^{\mathrm{corr}} \) to obtain an intrinsic reordering of the matrix. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:sinkx}, the algorithm successfully reorganizes the randomly permuted matrix into a more regular and structured form. Moreover, a diffusion embedding of the affinity graph reveals the underlying geometry of the spatial domain \( X \) and the frequency domain \( K \).

