    \label{fig:sin_aff}
\end{figure}


\textit{Kernel Factorization.} Algorithm~\ref{alg:compression} is applied to three versions of the matrix: the randomly permuted matrix, the reorganized matrix by our proposed method, and the matrix using the natural order of $k$ and $x_i$. For the butterfly factorization, we initiate the skeletonization process at the finest level \( \ell \) of the tree \( T_X \) such that the maximum number of columns per block satisfies \( \max_k |V_k^\ell| \leq 32 \).
 The precision of each interpolative decomposition is fixed at \( \varepsilon = 10^{-10} \). For the factorization by eGHWT, we fix  \(\varepsilon = 5 \times 10^{-2} \). 

The bottom-right panel of Figure~\ref{fig:sinkx} shows the average reconstruction error using eGHWT over randomly generated test functions, plotted as a function of the coefficient ratio retained in the approximation. This ratio is given by \( n / (2N^2) \times 100\% \), where \( n \) denotes the number of retained GHWT coefficients.
Among the three settings, the matrix with natural ordering and the reorganized matrix yield similar reconstruction error for a given coefficient ratio. In contrast, the randomly permuted matrix exhibits significantly higher error when using the same percentage of coefficients.

In Table~\ref{table:sinkx2},
we report the evalutation of facorization and kernel application under both butterfly and eGHWT. 
For both factorization approaches, our geometry-adaptive method achieves compression performance on the reorganized matrix that is comparable to that of the matrix organized by the natural order and significantly outperforms the compression applied to the unprocessed, randomly permuted matrix. For small values of \( N \), the memory usage of the compressed representations may initially exceed that of the original matrix memory due to the storage of hierarchical tree structures and index information in sparse formats. However, as \( N \) increases, both factorizations yield compressed forms with lower memory usage than the full kernel matrix.
The butterfly factorization, originally developed for oscillatory kernels, achieves high-precision approximation while maintaining both memory usage and kernel application complexity on the order of \( O(N \log N) \).  
The eGHWT factorization is based on constructing localized, data-adaptive wavelet-like basis functions over learned hierarchical trees, capturing smooth or structured patterns with reduced complexity. eGHWT supports fast kernel application and, based on empirical curve fitting at fixed precision, achieves memory usage scaling approximately as \( O(N^{1.5}) \).


