\textit{Kernel Factorization.}
We consider \( \nu = 0, 1, \) and \( 10 \) to evaluate the performance of our proposed method for the potential operator and for the acoustic wave operator at both low and high frequencies. For each setup, we apply Algorithm~\ref{alg:compression} to factorize the corresponding kernel.
For the butterfly factorization, we initiate the skeletonization process at the finest level \( \ell \) of the tree \( T_X \) such that the maximum number of columns per block satisfies \( \max_k |V_k^\ell| \leq 16 \). The precision of each interpolative decomposition (ID) is fixed at \( \varepsilon = 10^{-10} \) for all values of \( \nu \). For the eGHWT-based factorization, the threshold is set to \( \varepsilon = 10^{-3} \) for the potential operator (\( \nu = 0 \)) and \( \varepsilon = 10^{-2} \) for the acoustic wave kernels (\( \nu = 1 \) and \( \nu = 10 \)).

In Tables~\ref{table:Green Function}, \ref{table:HK_ghwt}, and \ref{table:HK_ghwt10}, we report the performance of the factorizations and the corresponding kernel applications for \( \nu = 0, 1, \) and \( 10 \), respectively, across varying sample sizes \( N \).
For both factorization approaches, our geometry-adaptive method achieves significantly better compression performance on the reorganized matrix compared to the unorganized input. As \( N \) increases, both methods produce compressed representations with lower memory usage than the full kernel matrix.
The butterfly factorization provides high-precision approximation while maintaining both memory usage and application complexity on the order of \( O(N \log N) \). Since the kernels considered are relatively smooth compared to the Fourier case, the eGHWT factorization is particularly effective at capturing their structure using localized, data-adaptive wavelet-like bases. Under fixed precision, the memory usage scales better than \( O(N \log N) \), and kernel application remains efficient at \( O(N \log N) \) due to the GHWT transform.



