Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently demonstrated remarkable code generation abilities~\cite{chen2021evaluatinglargelanguagemodels,zan2023largelanguagemodelsmeet,rozière2024codellamaopenfoundation,deepseek-coder,hui2024qwen2,codegemmateam2024codegemmaopencodemodels,openai2024gpt4technicalreport,jiang2024surveylargelanguagemodels,geminiteam2025geminifamilyhighlycapable,qwen3}. This capacity enables LLMs to solve data science-related tasks by calling external libraries in the generated code~\cite{lai2022ds1000naturalreliablebenchmark,wang2025largelanguagemodelsreplace,hong2025data}. However, the APIs of external libraries are updated very frequently, while the model still has outdated information in their parameters~\cite{wu2024versicodeversioncontrollablecodegeneration,islah2024gitchameleonunmaskingversionswitchingcapabilities,wang2025llmsmeetlibraryevolution,yao2023editing,liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting}. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:overview}-top, the LLM released before would generate code containing outdated APIs, leading to task failure in the up-to-date environment. This poses a challenge for the application of LLMs in scenarios that require high-quality code generation, such as AI4Research~\cite{lu2024aiscientist,starace2025paperbenchevaluatingaisability}, Software Engineering~\cite{zhang2023survey}, and Human-Computer Interaction~\cite{zhang2024unifying}.

The root cause of these challenges lies in the fact that LLMs are trained on static datasets, making it difficult for them to adapt to dynamically evolving scenarios such as API updates. While supervised fine-tuning (SFT) can partially alleviate this issue~\cite{liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting}, the high frequency of updates may result in exorbitant costs and catastrophic forgetting of previously learned knowledge~\cite{biderman2024loralearnsforgets,luo2025empiricalstudycatastrophicforgetting}. An alternative approach involves embedding updated information directly into the prompt as a dynamic knowledge supplement~\cite{liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting,wang2025llmsmeetlibraryevolution}. This method can be further enhanced by integrating it with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), offering greater potential in handling dynamic scenarios~\cite{lewis2021retrievalaugmentedgenerationknowledgeintensivenlp,gao2024retrievalaugmentedgenerationlargelanguage,gupta2024comprehensivesurveyretrievalaugmentedgeneration}. Furthermore, inputting updated knowledge through prompts avoids the risk of erasing other unrelated knowledge. Nevertheless, significant room for improvement remains in version-related code evaluation benchmarks, particularly for open-source code models~\cite{liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting}.
This may be attributed to the conflict between inherent (parameters) and external (prompt) knowledge, where LLMs tend to prioritize their internal knowledge.
In summary, while prompting is better suited for this scenario than SFT, it is necessary to address the challenge of knowledge conflicts.

Reinforcement Fine-Tuning (RFT) has been shown to enhance the model's ability to integrate retrieval in RAG systems, boosting response accuracy~\cite{jin2025searchr1trainingllmsreason}.
Inspired by this, we design ReCode, a rule-based \textbf{Re}inforcement learning for \textbf{Code} Update approach ~\cite{kaelbling1996reinforcementlearningsurvey,ghasemi2025comprehensivesurveyreinforcementlearning} to enhance the model's code migration capabilities when encountering conflicting information.
% \yunzhi{\textcolor{darkgreen}{[Done]} You need to highlight RL and reward design in this paragraph. I also think the logic is not coherent here.}
To take data science as an example, programmers first learn a specific version of the library, such as NumPy. Then, after being informed of the API updates, they can map the old version of the code in their minds to the new version. Our goal is to enable LLMs to use new APIs to complete tasks based on the updated information provided in the prompt. Similar to the Version-Aware Code Migration task in Versicode~\cite{wu2024versicodeversioncontrollablecodegeneration}, we train the model to migrate code from an old version to a new version based on the update information.
As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:overview}-bottom, we put the updated document in the prompt and fine-tune the model to better understand the prompt by using reinforcement learning based on the improved string similarity reward for code evaluation.

We evaluate the model's performance on CodeUpdateArena~\cite{liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting}, a more challenging task, where the model must solve practical tasks using API update information. Our experimental results indicate that ReCode significantly enhances LLMs' code generation capabilities in dynamic API scenarios. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:head}, \textbf{the trained Qwen2.5-Coder-7B model outperforms Qwen2.5-Coder-32B and achieves a higher Pass@1 score than DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B}. Additionally, ReCode's impact on the model's general code generation abilities is less pronounced than that of supervised fine-tuning (SFT).
In summary, our contributions are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item ReCode is the first to explore the application of rule-based RFT in dynamic API scenarios. Particularly, we combine the use of prompts with RFT.
    \item We construct a training dataset comprising approximately 2,000 data entries, specifically designed to train models in performing version migration based on updated information.
    \item Through extensive experiments and analyses, we highlight the potential of ReCode in code generation and knowledge update scenarios.
\end{itemize}

\section{API Knowledge Update}
\label{sec:api-kn-up}

\textbf{Problem formula}.
During the pre-training phase, the LLM accumulates code knowledge within its parameter $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ via an autoregressive approach~\cite{yenduri2023generativepretrainedtransformercomprehensive}. 
However, $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ remains static post-pre-training and thus fails to incorporate subsequent API updates. Consequently, when tasked with question $q$, the model may produce code containing outdated APIs: $\text{\textit{code with outdated API}} \gets P_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\cdot | q)$.

One basic strategy is to embed updated API information within the prompt $c_{update}$.
The model then generates code based on both the question $q$ and the embedded update details: $\text{\textit{code}} \gets P_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\cdot | q, c)$.
Nevertheless, this method introduces potential conflicts between the model's internal knowledge (housed in $\boldsymbol{\theta}$) and external information (provided in the prompt $c$).
Such conflicts can lead to the model overlooking the updated API details in $c$, even when additional updates are supplied.
Unlike directly writing the updated knowledge into the model's parameter $\boldsymbol{\theta}$, we employ reinforcement learning to fine-tune $\boldsymbol{\theta} \xrightarrow{} \boldsymbol{\theta}'$, enabling the model to more effectively leverage the updated API information presented in the prompt. This refined approach allows the model to generate code that better aligns with current API standards: $\text{\textit{update code}} \gets P_{\boldsymbol{\theta}'}(\cdot | q, c)$.

\noindent \textbf{CodeUpdateArena}.
CodeUpdateArena~\cite{liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting} is designed to evaluate the ability of LLMs to handle API updates.
The dataset comprises 670 program synthesis tasks, covering updates to 54 functions across seven different Python packages.
Unlike other datasets, CodeUpdateArena is a synthetic dataset generated with LLM assistance.
It prevents overlap with the model's training data and allows for the assessment of LLMs' adaptability to completely new updates.
In addition, each entry in the dataset includes at least three test cases that can be directly executed for verification.

\section{ReCode}

\subsection{Overview}
\label{sec:overview}

As we described in Section~\ref{sec:intro}, providing API update information to LLMs via prompts is the most promising solution, as it is more aligned with the behavior of human programmers and can be seamlessly integrated with RAG.
However, current LLMs usually miss the information provided in the instructions.
Our goal is to enhance the model's ability to follow the updated information provided in the prompt.

\textbf{Training}.
For training, we train models to perform version migration based on updated information.
As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:data_collect}-right, given a data entry $e_i=[d_i,v_i,u_i,c_i^{(old)},c_i^{(target)}]$, which corresponds to [\textit{Dependency, Target Version, Update Info, Old Code, and Target Code}], the input of training task is $x_i = [d_i,v_i,u_i,c_i^{(old)}]$ and the output is $y=c_i^{(target)}$.
The actual prompts with templates we used are provided in the Appendix~\ref{app:prompt}.
We provide a detailed discussion of the training dataset construction in Section~\ref{sec:data}.
Moreover, we detail the rewards utilized in the training process in Section~\ref{sec:reward-design}.

\textbf{Testing}.
During testing, each piece of data in CodeUpdateArena~\cite{liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting} includes: Dependency and API update doc $u_i$, a real-world question $q_i$, and a function signature to be implemented $s_i$.
The test task requires the model to generate the complete function code for $s_i$ based on the above three pieces of information.
Since the dataset provides test cases, we use Pass@$k$ as the evaluation metric.
The prompts with templates are also provided in the Appendix~\ref{app:prompt}.

