\subsection{Reward Design}
\label{sec:reward-design}

Similar to the existing works~\cite{deepseekai2025deepseekr1incentivizingreasoningcapability,xie2025logicrlunleashingllmreasoning}, the reward in ReCode consists of two parts: format and correctness.

\textbf{Format Reward}.
We hope that the output of our model meets the format: 

\textit{<think>...</think><answer>...</answer>}.

\noindent That is, to output the thinking process within the <think> tag, and to output the target code within the <answer> tag.
The format reward is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    R_{format}(x)=\left\{
    \begin{matrix}
+1,& \text{if x meets the format}  \\
-1,& \text{else} 
\end{matrix}\right.
\end{split}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $x$ is the output of the model.

\textbf{Correctness Reward}.
Code, unlike a math problem~\cite{hendrycks2021measuringmathematicalproblemsolving,liu2024mathbenchevaluatingtheoryapplication,chernyshev2025umath}, does not have a verifiable, unique standard answer. When improving coding skills, code quality can be verified by the pass rate of test cases~\cite{code-r1}. However, we argue that the pass rate of test cases is not a suitable reward metric for code migration task. Our training objective is to migrate correctly generated code to a new version, with the focus on ``migration'' rather than the inherent correctness of the code itself. 
Following previous works~\cite{wu2024versicodeversioncontrollablecodegeneration,wang2025llmsmeetlibraryevolution}, we use string matching metrics to evaluate the code's cross-version migration capability:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Edit Similarity (ES)}: ES assesses the similarity between predicted completions and target codes by analyzing the edit operations needed to transform one into the other.
    \item \textbf{Exact Match (EM)}: EM calculates the rate at which predicted completions exactly match the target codes after normalizing return values.
\end{itemize}
Versicode~\cite{wu2024versicodeversioncontrollablecodegeneration} introduced the Critical Diff Check (CDC) metric by adding rules based on EM. However, since it only considered API name updates, CDC is not applicable to diverse API updates. Drawing on Logic-RL's~\cite{xie2025logicrlunleashingllmreasoning} reward mechanisms, we incorporated code syntax checking into the string matching metrics, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:rft}.
Taking EM as an example:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    {EM^*}(x)=\left\{
    \begin{matrix}
+2.0,& \text{if x match the target}  \\
-1.5,& \text{elif x is syntactically valid} \\
-2.0,& \text{else}
\end{matrix}\right.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Similarly, $ES^*$ is:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    {ES^*}(x)=\left\{
    \begin{matrix}
-2.0, \text{if x isn't syntactically valid}  \\
ES(x)\times3.5-1.5, \text{else}
\end{matrix}\right.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The reward range when the syntax is correct is also $[-1.5, 2.0]$.
Our reward is applicable to any reinforcement learning algorithm that uses policy gradient updates. 
In our experiments, we select GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmathpushinglimitsmathematical} and its modified version, DAPO~\cite{yu2025dapoopensourcellmreinforcement}, as the training algorithms.

\section{Experiment}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Model}.
In our experiments, we utilize two code models to evaluate our method: Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct \cite{hui2024qwen2} and DeepSeek-v1.5-Coder-7B-Instruct \cite{deepseek-coder}. Both models demonstrate superior performance among models with fewer than 10B parameters. Notably, instruction-tuned models are found to retain R1 characteristics \cite{xie2025logicrlunleashingllmreasoning}.
Additionally, in the experimental section, we employ Qwen2.5-Coder-Instruct-32B \cite{hui2024qwen2} and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B \cite{deepseekai2025deepseekr1incentivizingreasoningcapability} as baseline models. 
The performance metrics for these two models are obtained via the API of SiliconFlow\footnote{\url{https://www.siliconflow.cn/}}.

\noindent \textbf{Training}.
In our experiment, we utilize the DoRA algorithm~\cite{liu2024doraweightdecomposedlowrankadaptation} to update the model due to the computational limits. 
The hyperparameters are configured as $r=64,\alpha=64$.
For the RL component, both GRPO and DAPO are set with $G=8$, while in GRPO, the parameter $\beta$ is assigned a value of $0.001$.
The training procedure spans 5000 steps, targeting the training tasks with a batch size set at $8$ and a learning rate of $5\times10^{-5}$.
Specifically, the initial $150$ training steps incorporate a learning rate warm-up schedule, which is subsequently followed by a cosine schedule.

\noindent \textbf{Test Dataset and Metric}.
We select the CodeUpdateArena benchmark~\cite{liu2025codeupdatearenabenchmarkingknowledgeediting} as our test task and filter inaccurate cases from this public dataset\footnote{\url{https://github.com/leo-liuzy/CodeUpdateArena/blob/main/data/arena-ungrouped.jsonl}}. 
A detailed account of these errors is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:test-data}. We evaluate our corrected data using the \textbf{Pass@$k$} metric, with $k$ values of $1$ and $5$ in our experiments.
All tests are conducted using the inputs with updated information as described in Section~\ref{sec:overview}.
To examine how additional post-training affects the model's general code capabilities, we employed HumanEval+~\cite{chen2021codex,liu2023is}.

