\subsection{Case Study: the impact of RL?}

A natural question arises: Why does ReCode result in improvements? 
To explore this question, we divide the error cases before training into two categories. One category includes cases that are corrected after traing, and the other includes those that remain wrong.

\textbf{ReCode can help LLMs overcome their laziness in acquiring external knowledge}.
We observe that the pretrained model exhibits "laziness" regarding the updates in the prompt. Specifically, it either ignores the mentioned updated APIs or overlooks the new API parameters. Appendix~\ref{app:err-case-1} shows an erroneous case where the model disregards the updated API \textit{math.sin} and misinterprets the angle unit "gradian," producing incorrect code.

\begin{tcolorbox}[colframe = customblue, colback = customblue!5]
\textbf{Hypothesis}. \textit{When generating code, an LLM draws from two information sources: internal knowledge (parameters) and provided prompts. Similar to humans, the model tends to rely more on its internal knowledge.}
\end{tcolorbox}

\noindent This propensity limits improvements when using prompts as the sole information source in dynamic scenarios.
This is also one of the challenges faced by RAG systems.
\citealt{longpre2022entitybasedknowledgeconflictsquestion} found that when encountering conflicting information between context and internal knowledge, QA models tend to rely on internal knowledge.
\citealt{xu2024knowledgeconflictsllmssurvey} emphasized that knowledge obsolescence can lead to a conflict between parameters and prompts.
Fortunately, ReCode corrects these issues, as demonstrated in Appendix~\ref{app:correct-case}, where the model accurately uses the updated API information.
ReCode enables the LLM to overcome its tendency to be ``lazy'' in utilizing external knowledge.

\textbf{The capacity to address problems is fundamentally constrained by the pre-trained model}. 
Most uncorrected cases are unrelated to API updates. Appendix~\ref{app:error-case-2} shows a case where the model is supposed to develop a pendulum simulation program. It ignores the physical quantities in the parameters and fails to describe the physical laws as required in the prompt. Unsurprisingly, even after training, the model still couldn't write this function correctly under the condition of Pass@$1$. Our method doesn't focus on enhancing physical reasoning abilities. Thus, Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct, which originally lacked this ability, still couldn't complete the task after training.

\subsection{Evaluation under Complex Scenarios}

Since our training and testing tasks only involve a single API update at a time, we explore two more complex scenarios:

\textbf{Multiple updates to a single API}. \textit{torch.gels()} was deprecated in version 1.2 in favor of \textit{torch.lstsq()}. Later, \textit{torch.lstsq()} was also deprecated in version 1.9, replaced by \textit{torch.linalg.lstsq()}. To update code from \textit{torch.gels} to \textit{torch.linalg.lstsq}, one could do it in two steps following the version sequence. However, as shown in Appendix~\ref{app:mul-app}, with update-aware prompting, the model can directly migrate from \textit{torch.gels} to \textit{torch.linalg.lstsq} without going through \textit{torch.lstsq}, which is more align with human programmers' behavior.

