Moreover, LLM responses are often wrapped in long, unnecessary explanations, resulting in computational inefficiency. While it is intuitive to think that a complicated question would require more steps, hence more tokens to reach a solution, recent works show that model responses tend to be lengthy even for simple arithmetic \cite{chen2025think23overthinkingo1like}, and LLMs often get stuck in "inefficient overthinking" \cite{illusion-of-thinking}. Long reasoning chains are also observed to lead to incorrect answers \cite{ha2025pensezdatabetterreasoning}. To mitigate this issue, recent approaches address overthinking by limiting the number of reasoning steps or redesigning prompts \cite{sui2025stopoverthinkingsurveyefficient}. The enormous amount of work in this area calls for a robust and efficient evaluation and benchmarking tool that can effectively diagnose overthinking.

To the best of our knowledge, no existing tool provides a comprehensive and extensible framework for evaluating LLMs on basic mathematical reasoning without relying on static benchmark datasets. This is crucial because: first, the mathematical reasoning tasks we focus on are fundamental operations rather than complex problem solving, unlike MATH \cite{hendrycksmath2021} or HLE \cite{phan2025humanitysexam} benchmarks; second, we avoid static benchmark datasets, which prevents memorization and provides a clean assessment of reasoning capabilities; and third, our evaluation framework is designed to be fast and flexible for practical deployment. Furthermore, commonly used evaluation methods, such as LLMs as a judge, are costly and do not generalize well across tasks \cite{gu2025surveyllmasajudge}. Most existing frameworks focus exclusively on accuracy metrics while overlooking computational efficiency and reasoning verbosity, thereby failing to capture the accuracy-efficiency tradeoff that is essential for practical deployment.

To address these gaps, we present \our, a comprehensive benchmarking and diagnostic framework designed to stress-test basic mathematical reasoning and overthinking in LLMs. Our tool provides researchers and practitioners with: 1) \textbf{dynamic test generation} capabilities that create mathematical problems on-the-fly across 14 fundamental task types, preventing data contamination and ensuring fresh evaluation scenarios; 2) \textbf{robust answer parsing} through task-specific parsers that combine structured prompting and regular expressions, iteratively refined through analysis of responses across 29 models from major families; 3) \textbf{efficiency-aware evaluation metrics} that quantify overthinking through accuracy-vs-token-length tradeoffs, enabling identification of models that balance performance with computational efficiency; 4) \textbf{flexible backend support} integrating a fast vLLM-based \cite{kwon2023efficient} backend with auto-fallback to HuggingFace Transformers \cite{wolf-etal-2020-transformers}, ensuring broad model compatibility while maintaining high-throughput evaluation; and 5) \textbf{full configurability} allowing users to configure experimental and system hyperparameters, extend with custom tasks, and reproduce experiments with optional seeding for consistent results.


\our is distributed as a \textit{pip-installable package} with both \textbf{CLI and API access}, making it easily accessible for researchers and developers. The framework addresses critical limitations in current evaluation practices by: 1) providing \textit{cost-effective evaluation} through task-specific parsers rather than expensive LLM-as-a-judge approaches; 2) generating \textit{reproducible datasets} while avoiding contamination concerns; and 3) offering \textit{diagnostic capabilities} essential for understanding basic math reasoning patterns that remain invisible upon evaluating on traditional benchmarks.





\section{Related Works}

\paragraph{Math Reasoning} LLMs achieve high performance on datasets such as GSM8K, GSMPlus, and HARDMath, but recent work has shown that they are unable to perform on basic mathematical tasks at a similar level \cite{srivastava2025reasoningabilitysmalllanguage, yan2025phdlevelllmstrulygrasp, xu2024llm}. Recent benchmarks comprising simple numerical problems evaluate the generalizability of LLMs on basic math tasks \cite{li2025exposingnumeracygapsbenchmark, rahman2025largelanguagemodelsnumberland}. This has led to several works on improving the mathematical reasoning of LLMs. Graph of Thoughts \cite{Besta_2024} models the information generated by an LLM as an arbitrary graph, reporting improvement on sorting tasks. LogicPuzzleRL \cite{wong2025logicpuzzlerlcultivatingrobustmathematical} fine-tunes LLMs through reinforcement learning on a suite of custom logic puzzles, showing improvement over algebraic, geometric, and combinatorial tasks. However, these works do not provide comprehensive evaluation tools for basic mathematical tasks.

\paragraph{LLM Overthinking} Recent works demonstrate that LLMs tend to provide lengthy reasoning chains to very simple questions \cite{chen2025think23overthinkingo1like}. Overthinking can be mitigated using methods such as model-based, reasoning output-based, and input prompts-based efficient reasoning \cite{sui2025stopoverthinkingsurveyefficient}. Works such as ThoughtTerminator \cite{pu2025thoughtterminatorbenchmarkingcalibratingmitigating} and Self-Braking Tuning \cite{zhao2025letllmsbreakfree} have been shown to reduce overthinking. However, existing approaches focus on mitigation rather than systematic evaluation and diagnosis of overthinking behavior.


\paragraph{LLM Reasoning Evaluation} There has been an increase in the number of frameworks that evaluate different aspects of LLM reasoning. Think-Bench \cite{li2025thinkbenchevaluatingthinkingefficiency} evaluates thinking efficiency and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) quality in Large Reasoning Models (LRM) on mathematics, physics, and chemistry questions. LegalAgentBench \cite{li2024legalagentbenchevaluatingllmagents} evaluates reasoning in the Chinese Legal domain. Despite their strengths, existing toolkits lack an extensible and efficient framework for diagnosing basic math reasoning and overthinking in deterministic tasks. Most frameworks also rely on static datasets, which may suffer from data contamination or expensive LLM-as-a-judge evaluation methods. Addressing this gap, \our presents a focused benchmarking platform with 14 tasks, task-specific parsers, and on-the-fly data generation while explicitly avoiding costly LLM-as-a-judge evaluation.

\section{\our}

In this section, we present the structure and implementation of LLMThinkBench (Figure~\ref{fig:main}).


\subsection{Task Curation and Dataset Generation}
The dataset generation module generates reproducible mathematical evaluation datasets across 14 operations. To ensure comprehensive coverage, our framework organizes tasks into four categories as shown in Table~\ref{tab:task_suite}: basic operations, extremum detection, statistical operations, and counting tasks.

Given user-specified parameters $(N, F, [r_{\min}, r_{\max}], \mathcal{L})$ where $N$ represents sample count, $F$ denotes evaluation folds, and $\mathcal{L} = \{l_1, l_2, \ldots, l_k\}$ specifies list sizes, the system generates randomized test instances using seeded random number generators. For each task $\tau_i$ and fold $f_j$, we sample data points $\mathcal{D}_{i,j} = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\}$ ensuring reproducibility across different experimental runs. The generation process adapts to task-specific requirements. For list-based tasks like sorting and statistical operations, we sample $n$ integers from the specified range $[r_{\min}, r_{\max}]$ where $n \in \mathcal{L}$. For pair-based tasks like comparison and division, we generate two numbers with appropriate constraints (e.g., non-zero denominators for division). This systematic approach ensures comprehensive coverage while maintaining mathematical validity across all generated problems.


\subsection{Model Inference}

Once task generation is completed, the generated problems are passed to the model inference module for evaluation. The inference module implements a dual-backend architecture that prioritizes performance while ensuring broad compatibility across different model architectures. The system first attempts to use vLLM for high-throughput batched inference, automatically detecting model compatibility and hardware requirements. For each data point $d_k \in \mathcal{D}_{i,j}$, the system generates structured prompts following the format: "\textit{Task description} + \textit{Problem instance} + \textit{Your final answer must be in the format \textbackslash boxed\{answer\}}". The model then generates response $r_k = M(\text{prompt}(d_k); \theta)$ where $\theta = \{T, p, \text{max\_tokens}\}$ represents configurable inference parameters.


When vLLM encounters compatibility issues or hardware constraints, the system seamlessly falls back to HuggingFace Transformers without requiring user intervention. This dual-backend approach ensures that evaluation can proceed regardless of model architecture or system limitations. The module also implements token counting using a tiktoken module, model-specific tokenizers when available, and word-based estimation as fallback, ensuring accurate efficiency measurements across different tokenization schemes. The inference stage supports multi-GPU tensor parallelism with configurable memory utilization, enabling efficient evaluation of large models while providing detailed progress tracking and error handling throughout the process.

\subsection{Answer Extraction}

After model inference is completed, the system transitions to the answer extraction stage, where a robust parsing module automatically extracts final answers from diverse model response formats. This stage addresses one of the most critical challenges in automated LLM evaluation: reliably identifying correct answers despite significant variation in response formatting across different model families. Through systematic analysis of response patterns from 29 models across major families (Qwen-7, LLaMA-5, Phi-4, Mistral-6, Others-15+), we developed a hierarchical parsing system that handles over 50 distinct answer formats. The parsing pipeline implements priority-ordered extraction methods:

\begin{align}
\mathcal{P}(r_k) = \begin{cases}
\text{Extract}_{box}(r_k) & \text{if } \backslash\text{boxed}\{\} \text{ found} \\
\text{Extract}_{exp}(r_k) & \text{if explicit found} \\
\text{Extract}_{ctx}(r_k) & \text{if markdown found} \\
\text{Extract}_{fall}(r_k) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align}


Each extraction method implements task-specific validation logic to ensure extracted answers represent final results rather than problem restatements. For example, the sorting task parser validates that extracted lists contain the same elements as input with correct ordering: $\text{Valid}_{sort}(\mathcal{P}(r), d) = \text{sorted}(\text{Input}(d)) \stackrel{?}{=} \mathcal{P}(r)$. Similarly, arithmetic tasks filter out input numbers to prevent extraction of problem components rather than solutions.


