\subsection{Overthinking Analysis}

The final stage processes extracted answers to compute comprehensive efficiency metrics that quantify the accuracy-verbosity tradeoff. This module implements our \textbf{Overthinking Score} metric, which addresses critical limitations in traditional accuracy-only evaluation approaches. The Overthinking Score uses harmonic mean formulation to balance accuracy and computational efficiency:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}_i = \frac{2 \cdot A_i \cdot E_{t,i}}{A_i + E_{t,i}}
\end{equation}

where accuracy $A_i = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{k=1}^{N} \mathbb{I}[\mathcal{P}(r_k) = \text{GroundTruth}(d_k)]$ and token efficiency 

$E_{t,i} = 1 - \frac{\bar{T}_i - T_{\min}}{T_{\max} - T_{\min}}$ where $\bar{T}_i = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{k=1}^{N} |r_k|$.

\vspace{1em}

The harmonic mean formulation is motivated by F1-score principles \cite{yacouby2020probabilistic}, addressing the inherent imbalance between accuracy and efficiency metrics. Unlike arithmetic means that can be dominated by either component, the harmonic mean penalizes extreme imbalances: $\lim_{E_t \to 0} \mathcal{O} = 0$ and $\lim_{A \to 0} \mathcal{O} = 0$. This ensures that models achieving high accuracy through excessive verbosity receive lower efficiency scores than models with balanced performance.

The analysis module aggregates results across multiple folds using mean and standard deviation calculations, providing statistical robustness and confidence intervals for reported metrics. The final reporting system generates efficiency rankings, detailed performance breakdowns, and JSON exports for downstream analysis. This comprehensive approach provides actionable insights for model selection and deployment decisions in resource-constrained environments.



