\section{Results and Discussion}

We evaluated 53 language models across multiple families using \textbf{\our}, revealing \textit{striking contradictions} between standard benchmark performance and basic math reasoning. These findings expose systematic inefficiencies in modern LLMs and challenge core assumptions about model scaling and reasoning enhancement.




\subsection{Performance on Basic Math Reasoning}

\textbf{Models that excel on complex benchmarks like GSM8K, MATH often struggle with basic arithmetic tasks,} suggesting they rely more on pattern recognition than genuine mathematical understanding. We observed significant different between models' reported performance on standard benchmarks and their capabilities on basic arithmetic tasks. While many of these models achieve impressive scores on GSM8K \cite{srivastava2025reasoningabilitysmalllanguage, srivastava2025debatetrainevolveself} and similar benchmarks \footnote{\url{https://ctrl-gaurav.github.io/slms-reasoning-leaderboard.github.io/}}, their performance on LLMThinkBench evaluations drops. 


The Qwen3 family shows particularly interesting scaling behavior. The 14B model achieves 86.52\% accuracy with strong efficiency (0.725), but the larger 32B variant surprisingly drops to 84.13\% accuracy (results in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:model_performance}). This non-monotonic scaling suggests that larger models don't always improve on fundamental reasoning tasks, possibly due to overfitting to complex patterns while losing sight of basic operations. \textsc{LLMThinkBench's} evaluation across fundamental mathematical operations exposes these gaps that traditional benchmarks miss. 


\subsection{The Overthinking Problem}

\textbf{Reasoning models generate $\sim$18x more tokens than standard models while achieving worse accuracy, and their performance collapses when token generation is constrained.} We discovered systematic overthinking behavior across model families, especially in reasoning-enhanced variants as shown in Figure \ref{fig:results} (b). The Phi-4-reasoning models \cite{abdin2025phi4reasoningtechnicalreport} exemplify this issue, generating 6,780 tokens on average compared to 378 tokens for standard Phi-4, while achieving lower accuracy (69.54\% vs 78.92\%). When we constrained reasoning models to 1,024 tokens, their performance dropped dramatically. Phi-4-reasoning fell from 72.23\% to 53.48\% accuracy, while Phi-4-reasoning-plus dropped from 69.54\% to 44.33\% (see Appendix Table~\ref{tab:reasoning_model_performance}). Figure~\ref{fig:results} shows this relationship across different reasoning models. These results suggest that reasoning models have developed a dependency on verbose exploration rather than efficient problem-solving. They seem to "think out loud" extensively without necessarily thinking more effectively.



\subsection{Quantization Effects on LLM Reasoning}

\textbf{Math reasoning capabilities are robust to quantization, with larger models maintaining performance even under aggressive 4-bit compression while smaller models are more vulnerable.} Our quantization analysis reveals surprising resilience in mathematical reasoning. Most models maintain performance well under 8-bit quantization. The Qwen2.5-32B model achieved 73.08\% accuracy under 8-bit quantization compared to 72.90\% at full precision (see Appendix Table~\ref{tab:quantized_model_performance}). However, 4-bit quantization shows more complex patterns. Larger models (32B, 72B) handle aggressive quantization remarkably well, while smaller models suffer more degradation. The 0.5B model drops from 21.31\% to 12.77\% accuracy under 4-bit quantization. Figure~\ref{fig:results} illustrates these size-dependent effects. This size-dependent resilience suggests that larger models have more redundant pathways for mathematical reasoning, making them more robust to precision reduction. This finding has important implications for deploying efficient models in resource-constrained environments.


\subsection{Model Size, Verbosity, and Accuracy Relationships}

\textbf{Larger models don't always perform better on basic reasoning tasks, and the most efficient models balance accuracy with response conciseness rather than simply scaling up parameters.} Our analysis reveals complex relationships between model size, response length, and accuracy that challenge simple assumptions about model performance. Figure~\ref{fig:results} (a) and also figure \ref{fig:lb3} in Appendix \ref{Leaderboard: ref} shows these relationships across different model families. Interestingly, some smaller models achieve better efficiency scores than their larger counterparts. The Llama-3.1-70B model achieves strong efficiency (0.691) despite its size. The overthinking score metric we introduce helps identify models that balance accuracy with response conciseness. Models with high efficiency scores solve problems correctly without unnecessary verbosity, suggesting they have learned more direct reasoning pathways.





\subsection{LLMThinkBench as a Diagnostic Tool}

Our framework addresses key limitations in current evaluation practices. Unlike expensive LLM-as-a-judge approaches, \textbf{\textsc{LLMThinkBench}} provides \textbf{cost-effective evaluation} through task-specific parsers validated across diverse model families. More \our live results and leaderboard snapshots are shown in Figure \ref{fig:combined} and full view in Appendix \ref{Leaderboard: ref}. The tool generates reproducible datasets while avoiding contamination concerns. The framework's modular design allows easy extension with new tasks and metrics. Most importantly, \textbf{\textsc{LLMThinkBench}} provides diagnostic capabilities needed to understand and improve fundamental reasoning in language models. Our results demonstrate that \textbf{\textsc{LLMThinkBench}} fills an essential gap in the evaluation ecosystem, providing the diagnostic tools necessary for more principled development of reasoning-capable language models.




