\section{Conclusion}
% \xw{The conclusion can never be too short. If you lack space, you can always shorten the conclusion.}
We developed LLMThinkBench, a robust evaluation framework for assessing basic mathematical reasoning and overthinking behavior in language models. Using \textbf{\textsc{LLMThinkBench}}, we evaluated 53 model which revealed fundamental gaps between benchmark performance and basic mathematical reasoning capabilities. \our addresses critical limitations in existing evaluation frameworks and provides essential diagnostic capabilities for understanding fundamental reasoning patterns invisible to traditional benchmarks, enabling more informed model development and deployment decisions.











\section*{Limitations}
\textbf{1)} \textbf{\our} focuses exclusively on basic mathematical operations and may not capture more complex reasoning required in real-world scenarios. \textbf{2)} The automated evaluation enables scalable assessment, though human studies could provide additional insights into metric interpretability. \textbf{3)} While dynamic test generation prevents memorization, models may still exploit learned mathematical patterns. \textbf{4)} The framework's effectiveness depends on task-specific parsers that require updates as model response formats evolve.

\clearpage

\section*{Ethics Statement}
\textbf{\our} is designed as an evaluation tool that does not generate harmful content or pose direct ethical risks. The framework evaluates publicly available language models on mathematical tasks without collecting personal data or requiring human annotations that could raise privacy concerns. All evaluated models are used according to their respective licensing terms, and our tool respects model usage policies. The evaluation results are intended to provide objective insights into model capabilities and should not be used to make discriminatory decisions about individuals or groups. We encourage responsible use of evaluation results and acknowledge that performance on basic mathematical tasks represents only one dimension of model capability.



